/* generated by mg_build.py: games replayed with python-chess; arena choices checked with Stockfish 16 */
window.TACTICS = {"patterns":{"skeleton":{"name":"Pawn skeleton","fam":"terrain","hint":"Pawns never move back. Where is the pawn majority, and which side do the pawn chains point at? Play there."},"outpost":{"name":"Outposts","fam":"terrain","hint":"Find a square no enemy pawn can ever attack. Which piece can reach it?"},"fit":{"name":"Pieces fit pawns","fam":"army","hint":"Count the pawns on each bishop's colour. Keep the piece that fits the structure; trade the one that doesn't."},"files":{"name":"Open files & 7th","fam":"army","hint":"Rooks need open lines. Where is the open file, and can a rook reach the 7th (or 6th) rank?"},"worst":{"name":"Worst piece","fam":"army","hint":"Point at your least useful piece (sometimes it's the king). Give it a job."},"proph":{"name":"Prophylaxis","fam":"mind","hint":"First ask: what does my opponent want to play next? Stop that."},"two":{"name":"Two weaknesses","fam":"mind","hint":"One weakness can be defended. Open a second front far away from the first."}},"order":["skeleton","outpost","fit","files","worst","proph","two"],"noun":"principle","famColors":{"terrain":"var(--guard)","army":"var(--linec)","mind":"var(--clock)"},"famNames":{"terrain":"the terrain","army":"the army","mind":"the mind"},"guessTail":"Notice how {fam} decides each move.","storeKey":"dml-chess-mg-arena","items":[{"id":"h-skeleton","pattern":"skeleton","kind":"hero","title":"The Pawn Move That Can't Be Taken Back","src":"Botvinnik – Capablanca · AVRO tournament, Netherlands 1938","fen":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/np3n2/2pp4/P2P4/2PQP3/1B2NPPP/R4RK1 b - - 2 14","orient":"b","n0":"<p>José Raúl Capablanca, the former world champion, was famous for never seeming to calculate. He just <i>knew</i> where pieces belonged. Mikhail Botvinnik was the young Soviet scientist of chess, who would become world champion ten years later.</p><p>Look at the pawns, not the pieces. In the centre the pawns are locked in tension: White's d4 against Black's d5, and Black's c5 pawn pressing on d4. Capablanca is about to make one pawn move that decides the whole game.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["c5","d4"],"steps":[{"u":"c5c4","s":"c4","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/np3n2/3p4/P1pP4/2PQP3/1B2NPPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 15","n":"<p><span class='mv'>14...c4?!</span> Capablanca releases the tension. It looks logical: Black gets a 3-against-2 pawn majority on the queenside, and White's c3 pawn is frozen.</p><p>But <b>a pawn move can never be taken back</b>. With c4 played, the centre is no longer contested. White now has a free hand there: <b>d4, e3 and f-pawn versus d5</b>, a central majority with a simple plan: <b>f3, e4, e5, f4, f5</b>.</p>","a":[{"u":"d4d5","k":"b"},{"u":"e3e4","k":"a"},{"u":"f2f3","k":"a"}],"m":["e3","f2","d4"]},{"u":"d3c2","s":"Qc2","by":"w","f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/np3n2/3p4/P1pP4/2P1P3/1BQ1NPPP/R4RK1 b - - 1 15","n":"<p>15.Qc2. Botvinnik doesn't hurry. Every white piece will be aimed at that plan.</p>"},{"u":"a6b8","s":"Nb8","by":"b","f":"rn2r1k1/p2q1ppp/1p3n2/3p4/P1pP4/2P1P3/1BQ1NPPP/R4RK1 w - - 2 16","n":"<p>15...Nb8. Capablanca's knight begins a long journey (a6-b8-c6-a5-b3) toward White's weak a4 pawn. He's playing on <i>his</i> side of the board.</p>"},{"u":"a1e1","s":"Rae1","by":"w","f":"rn2r1k1/p2q1ppp/1p3n2/3p4/P1pP4/2P1P3/1BQ1NPPP/4RRK1 b - - 3 16","n":"<p>16.Rae1. The rook goes to the e-file, behind the pawn that will advance. Rooks belong behind the pawns they support.</p>"},{"u":"b8c6","s":"Nc6","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/1pn2n2/3p4/P1pP4/2P1P3/1BQ1NPPP/4RRK1 w - - 4 17"},{"u":"e2g3","s":"Ng3","by":"w","f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/1pn2n2/3p4/P1pP4/2P1P1N1/1BQ2PPP/4RRK1 b - - 5 17","n":"<p>17.Ng3. The knight leaves e2 to clear the way for e3-e4 and to watch f5 and e4.</p>"},{"u":"c6a5","s":"Na5","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/1p3n2/n2p4/P1pP4/2P1P1N1/1BQ2PPP/4RRK1 w - - 6 18"},{"u":"f2f3","s":"f3","by":"w","f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/1p3n2/n2p4/P1pP4/2P1PPN1/1BQ3PP/4RRK1 b - - 0 18","n":"<p>18.f3. Preparing e4 with total support. Notice: White is not attacking anything yet. He is building a pawn wave.</p>","a":[{"u":"e3e4","k":"a"}]},{"u":"a5b3","s":"Nb3","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/1p3n2/3p4/P1pP4/1nP1PPN1/1BQ3PP/4RRK1 w - - 1 19","n":"<p>18...Nb3. The black knight has arrived and the a4 pawn will fall. Capablanca wins a pawn on the queenside...</p><p><b>Now it's your move as White.</b> Where do the pawns tell you to play? You'll make this decision yourself in the Arena below, and then two more from this same game.</p>","a":[{"u":"b3a4","k":"b"},{"u":"e3e4","k":"a"}]}],"why":"<p>Pawns are the only units that can't retreat, so every pawn move permanently redraws the map. When Capablanca played ...c4, the map said: <b>Black plays on the queenside, White plays in the centre and on the kingside</b>. Whoever's side of the map matters more wins, and the centre is next to the king.</p>","diff":0,"mate":false},{"id":"h-outpost","pattern":"outpost","kind":"hero","title":"The Octopus","src":"Karpov – Kasparov · World Championship, Moscow 1985, game 16","fen":"r2qr1k1/1p3ppp/p4n2/2bP1bB1/1n6/N1N2B2/PP1Q1PPP/R4RK1 b - - 10 15","orient":"b","n0":"<p>Garry Kasparov, 22, is challenging Anatoly Karpov for the world title. Earlier in this game he <b>gave away a pawn</b> on purpose. White's extra pawn sits on d5. What did Kasparov get for it?</p><p>Look at the square <b>d3</b>. White has no c-pawn and no e-pawn. No white pawn can <i>ever</i> attack d3 again.</p>","a0":[{"u":"f5d3","k":"c"}],"m0":["d3"],"steps":[{"u":"b7b5","s":"b5","by":"b","f":"r2qr1k1/5ppp/p4n2/1pbP1bB1/1n6/N1N2B2/PP1Q1PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 16","n":"<p>15...b5. First Kasparov takes squares away from White's knights: a4-b5 and c4 are now covered.</p>"},{"u":"a1d1","s":"Rad1","by":"w","f":"r2qr1k1/5ppp/p4n2/1pbP1bB1/1n6/N1N2B2/PP1Q1PPP/3R1RK1 b - - 1 16","n":"<p>16.Rad1.</p>"},{"u":"b4d3","s":"Nd3","by":"b","f":"r2qr1k1/5ppp/p4n2/1pbP1bB1/8/N1Nn1B2/PP1Q1PPP/3R1RK1 w - - 2 17","n":"<p><span class='mv'>16...Nd3!</span> The knight lands in the hole. It's protected by the f5-bishop, it can't be chased by a pawn, and from d3 it reaches into b2, c1, e1, f2, f4. Commentators called it <b>the octopus</b>.</p>","a":[{"u":"d3b2","k":"a"},{"u":"d3f2","k":"a"},{"u":"d3e1","k":"a"},{"u":"d3c1","k":"a"}]},{"u":"a3b1","s":"Nab1","by":"w","f":"r2qr1k1/5ppp/p4n2/1pbP1bB1/8/2Nn1B2/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 b - - 3 17","n":"<p>17.Nab1. Look at White's knights. One retreats to b1 just to watch the octopus...</p>"},{"u":"h7h6","s":"h6","by":"b","f":"r2qr1k1/5pp1/p4n1p/1pbP1bB1/8/2Nn1B2/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 w - - 0 18"},{"u":"g5h4","s":"Bh4","by":"w","f":"r2qr1k1/5pp1/p4n1p/1pbP1b2/7B/2Nn1B2/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 b - - 1 18"},{"u":"b5b4","s":"b4","by":"b","f":"r2qr1k1/5pp1/p4n1p/2bP1b2/1p5B/2Nn1B2/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 w - - 0 19","n":"<p>18...b4! The pawn kicks the other white knight away from c3...</p>"},{"u":"c3a4","s":"Na4","by":"w","f":"r2qr1k1/5pp1/p4n1p/2bP1b2/Np5B/3n1B2/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 b - - 1 19","n":"<p>19.Na4. ...and it has to go to the rim. White's knights are on a4 and b1, guarding nothing important.</p>","a":[{"u":"a4b2","k":"b"}]},{"u":"c5d6","s":"Bd6","by":"b","f":"r2qr1k1/5pp1/p2b1n1p/3P1b2/Np5B/3n1B2/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 w - - 2 20"},{"u":"h4g3","s":"Bg3","by":"w","f":"r2qr1k1/5pp1/p2b1n1p/3P1b2/Np6/3n1BB1/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 b - - 3 20"},{"u":"a8c8","s":"Rc8","by":"b","f":"2rqr1k1/5pp1/p2b1n1p/3P1b2/Np6/3n1BB1/PP1Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 w - - 4 21"},{"u":"b2b3","s":"b3","by":"w","f":"2rqr1k1/5pp1/p2b1n1p/3P1b2/Np6/1P1n1BB1/P2Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 b - - 0 21"},{"u":"g7g5","s":"g5","by":"b","f":"2rqr1k1/5p2/p2b1n1p/3P1bp1/Np6/1P1n1BB1/P2Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 w - - 0 22"},{"u":"g3d6","s":"Bxd6","by":"w","f":"2rqr1k1/5p2/p2B1n1p/3P1bp1/Np6/1P1n1B2/P2Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 b - - 0 22"},{"u":"d8d6","s":"Qxd6","by":"b","f":"2r1r1k1/5p2/p2q1n1p/3P1bp1/Np6/1P1n1B2/P2Q1PPP/1N1R1RK1 w - - 0 23","n":"<p>Kasparov's minor pieces stand on d3 and f5, deep in White's half. White's knights stand on a4 and b1. Kasparov is a pawn down and clearly better. The octopus stayed on d3 for 18 moves, and its final dance is in the case files.</p>","m":["a4","b1"]}],"why":"<p>Pawns attack only forwards and diagonally. Once the pawns that could cover a square are gone or have advanced past it, that square is a <b>permanent hole</b>. A piece standing there can only be removed by trading a piece for it, and a knight in a hole in the enemy half is often worth more than a rook.</p>","diff":0,"mate":false},{"id":"h-fit","pattern":"fit","kind":"hero","title":"Trading the Beautiful Knight","src":"Fischer – Petrosian · Candidates final, Buenos Aires 1971, game 7","fen":"r4k2/4rppp/p3bn2/3p4/NP6/3B4/P4PPP/R3R1K1 w - - 1 19","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Bobby Fischer against Tigran Petrosian, the greatest defender of his era, nicknamed <i>Iron Tigran</i>. The queens are gone. Black has an isolated pawn on d5, and his remaining pawns (a6, d5) stand on <b>light squares</b>.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["a6","d5"],"steps":[{"u":"a4c5","s":"Nc5","by":"w","f":"r4k2/4rppp/p3bn2/2Np4/1P6/3B4/P4PPP/R3R1K1 b - - 2 19","n":"<p>19.Nc5. The knight jumps to a superb square, attacking the bishop and a6.</p>"},{"u":"e6c8","s":"Bc8","by":"b","f":"r1b2k2/4rppp/p4n2/2Np4/1P6/3B4/P4PPP/R3R1K1 w - - 3 20","n":"<p>19...Bc8. The bishop retreats to defend a6.</p>"},{"u":"f2f3","s":"f3","by":"w","f":"r1b2k2/4rppp/p4n2/2Np4/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/R3R1K1 b - - 0 20"},{"u":"e7a7","s":"Rea7","by":"b","f":"r1b2k2/r4ppp/p4n2/2Np4/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/R3R1K1 w - - 1 21"},{"u":"e1e5","s":"Re5","by":"w","f":"r1b2k2/r4ppp/p4n2/2NpR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/R5K1 b - - 2 21","n":"<p>21.Re5. The rook steps up to attack d5.</p>"},{"u":"c8d7","s":"Bd7","by":"b","f":"r4k2/r2b1ppp/p4n2/2NpR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/R5K1 w - - 3 22","n":"<p>21...Bd7. Black covers b5 and prepares ...Bb5 to trade off White's strong bishop.</p>"},{"u":"c5d7","s":"Nxd7+","by":"w","f":"r4k2/r2N1ppp/p4n2/3pR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/R5K1 b - - 0 22","n":"<p><span class='mv'>22.Nxd7+!</span> The room gasped. Fischer gives up his <b>beautiful</b> knight for Black's bishop. Why?</p><p>Count what's left: White's light-squared bishop against Black's knight. Black's pawns sit on light squares, so they're targets for the bishop, and with pawns on both sides of the board, a long-range bishop beats a short-legged knight. The knight was beautiful. The bishop <b>fits</b>.</p>","a":[{"u":"d3a6","k":"c"},{"u":"d3h7","k":"c"}],"m":["a6","d5"]},{"u":"a7d7","s":"Rxd7","by":"b","f":"r4k2/3r1ppp/p4n2/3pR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/R5K1 w - - 0 23","n":"<p>Forced.</p>"},{"u":"a1c1","s":"Rc1","by":"w","f":"r4k2/3r1ppp/p4n2/3pR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/2R3K1 b - - 1 23","n":"<p>23.Rc1. Now the open c-file.</p>"},{"u":"d7d6","s":"Rd6","by":"b","f":"r4k2/5ppp/p2r1n2/3pR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/2R3K1 w - - 2 24"},{"u":"c1c7","s":"Rc7","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2R2ppp/p2r1n2/3pR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/6K1 b - - 3 24","n":"<p><span class='mv'>24.Rc7</span> The rook reaches the 7th rank. Black's pieces are tied to defending. Fischer won this game and the match, and the next year he became world champion.</p>","a":[{"u":"c7f7","k":"a"}]}],"why":"<p>Minor pieces have no fixed value. Their value comes from the pawns around them. A bishop is <b>good</b> when its own pawns sit on the other colour and the board is open, and <b>bad</b> when its pawns block it. A knight needs outposts and closed positions. Keep the piece that fits the pawn structure, even if it means giving up the prettier one.</p>","diff":0,"mate":false},{"id":"h-files","pattern":"files","kind":"hero","title":"Alekhine's Gun","src":"Alekhine – Nimzowitsch · San Remo 1930","fen":"r4rk1/p3nqp1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R4RK1 w - - 0 20","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Alexander Alekhine, the world champion, against Aron Nimzowitsch, the great theorist (you'll meet him again in Prophylaxis). The centre is locked: White's pawns on d4 and e5 point toward the black king, Black's on d5 and f5.</p><p>On a locked board, rooks behind the pawns are useless. There is exactly one road into Black's camp: <b>the c-file</b>.</p>","a0":[{"u":"c1c8","k":"c"}],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"f1c1","s":"Rfc1","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/p3nqp1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R1R3K1 b - - 1 20","n":"<p>20.Rfc1. The first rook takes the road.</p>"},{"u":"f8c8","s":"Rfc8","by":"b","f":"r1r3k1/p3nqp1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R1R3K1 w - - 2 21"},{"u":"c1c2","s":"Rc2","by":"w","f":"r1r3k1/p3nqp1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/2RQ2PP/R5K1 b - - 3 21","n":"<p>21.Rc2. Making room for the second rook...</p>"},{"u":"f7e8","s":"Qe8","by":"b","f":"r1r1q1k1/p3n1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/2RQ2PP/R5K1 w - - 4 22"},{"u":"a1c1","s":"Rac1","by":"w","f":"r1r1q1k1/p3n1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/2RQ2PP/2R3K1 b - - 5 22","n":"<p>22.Rac1. Two rooks on the c-file.</p>"},{"u":"a8b8","s":"Rab8","by":"b","f":"1rr1q1k1/p3n1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/2RQ2PP/2R3K1 w - - 6 23"},{"u":"d2e3","s":"Qe3","by":"w","f":"1rr1q1k1/p3n1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/4QN2/2R3PP/2R3K1 b - - 7 23"},{"u":"c8c7","s":"Rc7","by":"b","f":"1r2q1k1/p1r1n1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/4QN2/2R3PP/2R3K1 w - - 8 24"},{"u":"c2c3","s":"Rc3","by":"w","f":"1r2q1k1/p1r1n1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R1QN2/6PP/2R3K1 b - - 9 24","n":"<p>24.Rc3. And now the queen joins: the rooks move up so the queen can go behind them.</p>"},{"u":"e8d7","s":"Qd7","by":"b","f":"1r4k1/p1rqn1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R1QN2/6PP/2R3K1 w - - 10 25"},{"u":"c1c2","s":"R1c2","by":"w","f":"1r4k1/p1rqn1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R1QN2/2R3PP/6K1 b - - 11 25"},{"u":"g8f8","s":"Kf8","by":"b","f":"1r3k2/p1rqn1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R1QN2/2R3PP/6K1 w - - 12 26"},{"u":"e3c1","s":"Qc1","by":"w","f":"1r3k2/p1rqn1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 b - - 13 26","n":"<p><span class='mv'>26.Qc1!</span> Queen behind two rooks, all on one file. This formation is called <b>Alekhine's gun</b>. The barrel points at c6 and c7.</p>","a":[{"u":"c1c3","k":"a"},{"u":"c3c6","k":"b"}],"m":["c6","c7"]},{"u":"b8c8","s":"Rbc8","by":"b","f":"2r2k2/p1rqn1p1/Ppn1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 w - - 14 27"},{"u":"b5a4","s":"Ba4","by":"w","f":"2r2k2/p1rqn1p1/Ppn1p2p/3pPp2/BP1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 b - - 15 27"},{"u":"b6b5","s":"b5","by":"b","f":"2r2k2/p1rqn1p1/P1n1p2p/1p1pPp2/BP1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 w - - 0 28"},{"u":"a4b5","s":"Bxb5","by":"w","f":"2r2k2/p1rqn1p1/P1n1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 b - - 0 28","n":"<p>28.Bxb5. White grabs a pawn. Black's pieces are glued to the c-file defence.</p>"},{"u":"f8e8","s":"Ke8","by":"b","f":"2r1k3/p1rqn1p1/P1n1p2p/1B1pPp2/1P1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 w - - 1 29"},{"u":"b5a4","s":"Ba4","by":"w","f":"2r1k3/p1rqn1p1/P1n1p2p/3pPp2/BP1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 b - - 2 29"},{"u":"e8d8","s":"Kd8","by":"b","f":"2rk4/p1rqn1p1/P1n1p2p/3pPp2/BP1P1P2/2R2N2/2R3PP/2Q3K1 w - - 3 30"},{"u":"h2h4","s":"h4","by":"w","f":"2rk4/p1rqn1p1/P1n1p2p/3pPp2/BP1P1P1P/2R2N2/2R3P1/2Q3K1 b - - 0 30","n":"<p><span class='mv'>30.h4!</span> A quiet pawn move, and Nimzowitsch resigned. Black is almost in <b>zugzwang</b>: every piece is stuck defending the c-file, and every move loses something.</p>"}],"why":"<p>Rooks are long-range pieces with no way past pawns, so they need <b>open files</b>. Whoever controls the only open file controls the only road in. Doubling (or tripling) on that file means the opponent can't contest it without losing material. The prize at the end of the road is the 7th rank, where enemy pawns can't be defended by other pawns.</p>","diff":0,"mate":false},{"id":"h-worst","pattern":"worst","kind":"hero","title":"The Long Walk of the Spanish Knight","src":"Karpov – Unzicker · Nice Olympiad 1974 (part I)","fen":"r1b2rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/npp1p3/3PP3/2P2N1P/PPB2PP1/RNBQR1K1 w - - 1 12","orient":"w","n0":"<p>A closed Ruy Lopez (Spanish opening), one of the slowest structures in chess. When nothing can be calculated, Siegbert Tarrasch's old rule takes over: <i>if one piece stands badly, the whole game stands badly</i>.</p><p>White's worst piece? The knight on <b>b1</b>. It has never moved and it's blocking the bishop on c1.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["b1"],"steps":[{"u":"b1d2","s":"Nbd2","by":"w","f":"r1b2rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/npp1p3/3PP3/2P2N1P/PPBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 b - - 2 12","n":"<p><span class='mv'>12.Nbd2</span> Step one of a famous route: b1 → d2 → f1 → g3.</p>","a":[{"u":"d2f1","k":"a"},{"u":"f1g3","k":"a"}]},{"u":"a5c6","s":"Nc6","by":"b","f":"r1b2rk1/2q1bppp/p1np1n2/1pp1p3/3PP3/2P2N1P/PPBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 w - - 3 13","n":"<p>12...Nc6. Watch Black's knight too. It's about to go on a sad journey of its own.</p>"},{"u":"d4d5","s":"d5","by":"w","f":"r1b2rk1/2q1bppp/p1np1n2/1ppPp3/4P3/2P2N1P/PPBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 b - - 0 13","n":"<p>13.d5. White closes the centre. The black knight is kicked.</p>"},{"u":"c6d8","s":"Nd8","by":"b","f":"r1bn1rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/1ppPp3/4P3/2P2N1P/PPBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 w - - 1 14","n":"<p>13...Nd8. Backwards.</p>"},{"u":"a2a4","s":"a4","by":"w","f":"r1bn1rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/1ppPp3/P3P3/2P2N1P/1PBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 b - - 0 14"},{"u":"a8b8","s":"Rb8","by":"b","f":"1rbn1rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/1ppPp3/P3P3/2P2N1P/1PBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 w - - 1 15"},{"u":"a4b5","s":"axb5","by":"w","f":"1rbn1rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/1PpPp3/4P3/2P2N1P/1PBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 b - - 0 15"},{"u":"a6b5","s":"axb5","by":"b","f":"1rbn1rk1/2q1bppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/4P3/2P2N1P/1PBN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 w - - 0 16"},{"u":"b2b4","s":"b4","by":"w","f":"1rbn1rk1/2q1bppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P2N1P/2BN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 b - - 0 16","n":"<p>16.b4. White gains space on the queenside.</p>"},{"u":"d8b7","s":"Nb7","by":"b","f":"1rb2rk1/1nq1bppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P2N1P/2BN1PP1/R1BQR1K1 w - - 1 17","n":"<p>16...Nb7. The black knight, which started on a5, is now on b7, looking at a wall of pawns. It will stay terrible for the whole game.</p>"},{"u":"d2f1","s":"Nf1","by":"w","f":"1rb2rk1/1nq1bppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P2N1P/2B2PP1/R1BQRNK1 b - - 2 17","n":"<p><span class='mv'>17.Nf1</span> Step two. The knight steps back to step forward.</p>"},{"u":"c8d7","s":"Bd7","by":"b","f":"1r3rk1/1nqbbppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P2N1P/2B2PP1/R1BQRNK1 w - - 3 18"},{"u":"c1e3","s":"Be3","by":"w","f":"1r3rk1/1nqbbppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P1BN1P/2B2PP1/R2QRNK1 b - - 4 18","n":"<p>18.Be3. With the knight gone from d2, the c1-bishop finally comes out.</p>"},{"u":"b8a8","s":"Ra8","by":"b","f":"r4rk1/1nqbbppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P1BN1P/2B2PP1/R2QRNK1 w - - 5 19"},{"u":"d1d2","s":"Qd2","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/1nqbbppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P1BN1P/2BQ1PP1/R3RNK1 b - - 6 19"},{"u":"f8c8","s":"Rfc8","by":"b","f":"r1r3k1/1nqbbppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2P1BN1P/2BQ1PP1/R3RNK1 w - - 7 20"},{"u":"c2d3","s":"Bd3","by":"w","f":"r1r3k1/1nqbbppp/3p1n2/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2PBBN1P/3Q1PP1/R3RNK1 b - - 8 20","n":"<p>20.Bd3. Even the bishop is re-routed to a better diagonal.</p>"},{"u":"g7g6","s":"g6","by":"b","f":"r1r3k1/1nqbbp1p/3p1np1/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2PBBN1P/3Q1PP1/R3RNK1 w - - 0 21"},{"u":"f1g3","s":"Ng3","by":"w","f":"r1r3k1/1nqbbp1p/3p1np1/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2PBBNNP/3Q1PP1/R3R1K1 b - - 1 21","n":"<p><span class='mv'>21.Ng3</span> Arrived. The knight that was asleep on b1 now eyes f5 and h5, the kingside squares where White will later strike.</p>","a":[{"u":"g3f5","k":"c"},{"u":"g3h5","k":"c"}]},{"u":"e7f8","s":"Bf8","by":"b","f":"r1r2bk1/1nqb1p1p/3p1np1/1ppPp3/1P2P3/2PBBNNP/3Q1PP1/R3R1K1 w - - 2 22","n":"<p>Compare the two knights that started on the queenside: White's on <b>g3</b> (active, eyeing f5 and h5), Black's on <b>b7</b> (blocked by its own pawns). That gap is the whole game. What Karpov does next is in Two Weaknesses.</p>","a":[{"u":"b7d6","k":"b"}],"m":["g3","b7"]}],"why":"<p>When the position is quiet, the question is not \"what can I attack?\" but \"<b>which of my pieces is doing nothing?</b>\" Give it a job, even if the route takes four moves. In closed positions you have the time; your opponent can't punish slowness through a locked centre.</p>","diff":0,"mate":false},{"id":"h-proph","pattern":"proph","kind":"hero","title":"The Immortal Zugzwang","src":"Sämisch – Nimzowitsch · Copenhagen 1923","fen":"r4rk1/3qbppp/p1b1p3/1p1p3n/3P4/1QN3PP/PP1BPPBK/2R2R2 b - - 4 16","orient":"b","n0":"<p>Aron Nimzowitsch wrote <i>My System</i>, one of the most influential chess books ever, and gave the world the word <b>prophylaxis</b>: before pursuing your own plan, stop your opponent's.</p><p>White's only freeing idea in this position is the central break <b>e2-e4</b>. Watch every black move.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["e4"],"steps":[{"u":"f7f5","s":"f5","by":"b","f":"r4rk1/3qb1pp/p1b1p3/1p1p1p1n/3P4/1QN3PP/PP1BPPBK/2R2R2 w - - 0 17","n":"<p><span class='mv'>16...f5!</span> The pawn clamps e4. White's freeing break now costs material.</p>","a":[{"u":"f5e4","k":"b"}],"m":["e4"]},{"u":"b3d1","s":"Qd1","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/3qb1pp/p1b1p3/1p1p1p1n/3P4/2N3PP/PP1BPPBK/2RQ1R2 b - - 1 17","n":"<p>17.Qd1.</p>"},{"u":"b5b4","s":"b4","by":"b","f":"r4rk1/3qb1pp/p1b1p3/3p1p1n/1p1P4/2N3PP/PP1BPPBK/2RQ1R2 w - - 0 18","n":"<p><span class='mv'>17...b4</span> Kicking the knight away from c3, where it supported e4.</p>"},{"u":"c3b1","s":"Nb1","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/3qb1pp/p1b1p3/3p1p1n/1p1P4/6PP/PP1BPPBK/1NRQ1R2 b - - 1 18","n":"<p>18.Nb1. The knight is sent home.</p>"},{"u":"c6b5","s":"Bb5","by":"b","f":"r4rk1/3qb1pp/p3p3/1b1p1p1n/1p1P4/6PP/PP1BPPBK/1NRQ1R2 w - - 2 19","n":"<p><span class='mv'>18...Bb5</span> The bishop hits e2, the pawn White wants to push. Black is restraining before attacking.</p>","a":[{"u":"b5e2","k":"a"}]},{"u":"f1g1","s":"Rg1","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/3qb1pp/p3p3/1b1p1p1n/1p1P4/6PP/PP1BPPBK/1NRQ2R1 b - - 3 19"},{"u":"e7d6","s":"Bd6","by":"b","f":"r4rk1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p1p1n/1p1P4/6PP/PP1BPPBK/1NRQ2R1 w - - 4 20"},{"u":"e2e4","s":"e4","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p1p1n/1p1PP3/6PP/PP1B1PBK/1NRQ2R1 b - - 0 20","n":"<p>20.e4. White finally pushes...</p>"},{"u":"f5e4","s":"fxe4","by":"b","f":"r4rk1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p3n/1p1Pp3/6PP/PP1B1PBK/1NRQ2R1 w - - 0 21","n":"<p><span class='mv'>20...fxe4!</span> ...and Nimzowitsch gives up his knight on h5 to keep the clamp and open the f-file.</p>"},{"u":"d1h5","s":"Qxh5","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p3Q/1p1Pp3/6PP/PP1B1PBK/1NR3R1 b - - 0 21"},{"u":"f8f2","s":"Rxf2","by":"b","f":"r5k1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p3Q/1p1Pp3/6PP/PP1B1rBK/1NR3R1 w - - 0 22","n":"<p>21...Rxf2. The rook lands on White's second rank.</p>"},{"u":"h5g5","s":"Qg5","by":"w","f":"r5k1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p2Q1/1p1Pp3/6PP/PP1B1rBK/1NR3R1 b - - 1 22"},{"u":"a8f8","s":"Raf8","by":"b","f":"5rk1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p2Q1/1p1Pp3/6PP/PP1B1rBK/1NR3R1 w - - 2 23"},{"u":"h2h1","s":"Kh1","by":"w","f":"5rk1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p2Q1/1p1Pp3/6PP/PP1B1rB1/1NR3RK b - - 3 23"},{"u":"f8f5","s":"R8f5","by":"b","f":"6k1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p1rQ1/1p1Pp3/6PP/PP1B1rB1/1NR3RK w - - 4 24"},{"u":"g5e3","s":"Qe3","by":"w","f":"6k1/3q2pp/p2bp3/1b1p1r2/1p1Pp3/4Q1PP/PP1B1rB1/1NR3RK b - - 5 24"},{"u":"b5d3","s":"Bd3","by":"b","f":"6k1/3q2pp/p2bp3/3p1r2/1p1Pp3/3bQ1PP/PP1B1rB1/1NR3RK w - - 6 25","n":"<p>24...Bd3. White's pieces have almost no safe moves left.</p>"},{"u":"c1e1","s":"Rce1","by":"w","f":"6k1/3q2pp/p2bp3/3p1r2/1p1Pp3/3bQ1PP/PP1B1rB1/1N2R1RK b - - 7 25"},{"u":"h7h6","s":"h6","by":"b","f":"6k1/3q2p1/p2bp2p/3p1r2/1p1Pp3/3bQ1PP/PP1B1rB1/1N2R1RK w - - 0 26","n":"<p><span class='mv'>25...h6!!</span> A pure waiting move. White is in <b>zugzwang</b>: every move he can make loses material or allows a decisive blow. Sämisch resigned. Nimzowitsch called it his immortal zugzwang game.</p>","a":[]}],"why":"<p>Every move changes what's possible for <b>both</b> players. Strong players ask \"what does he want?\" before \"what do I want?\". Restrain the opponent's freeing break first, and very often his position collapses by itself, because a position without a plan slowly runs out of good moves.</p>","diff":0,"mate":false},{"id":"h-two","pattern":"two","kind":"hero","title":"Open a Second Front","src":"Karpov – Unzicker · Nice Olympiad 1974 (part II)","fen":"r1rq1bk1/1n1b1p1p/3p1np1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P1BNNP/R2Q1PP1/1B2R1K1 w - - 2 24","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Back to Karpov (after 22.Ra2 c4 23.Bb1 Qd8). The queenside is White's first front: he owns the a-file. But Black can defend one front forever. Karpov needs a <b>second weakness</b>.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"e3a7","s":"Ba7","by":"w","f":"r1rq1bk1/Bn1b1p1p/3p1np1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R2Q1PP1/1B2R1K1 b - - 3 24","n":"<p><span class='mv'>24.Ba7!</span> The famous move. Karpov buries his own bishop on a7! It looks absurd, but it walls Black's rook off from the a-file and controls b8. Black can never challenge the file.</p>","a":[{"u":"a7b8","k":"a"},{"u":"a2a8","k":"c"}],"m":["a7"]},{"u":"f6e8","s":"Ne8","by":"b","f":"r1rqnbk1/Bn1b1p1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R2Q1PP1/1B2R1K1 w - - 4 25"},{"u":"b1c2","s":"Bc2","by":"w","f":"r1rqnbk1/Bn1b1p1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R1BQ1PP1/4R1K1 b - - 5 25"},{"u":"e8c7","s":"Nc7","by":"b","f":"r1rq1bk1/Bnnb1p1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R1BQ1PP1/4R1K1 w - - 6 26"},{"u":"e1a1","s":"Rea1","by":"w","f":"r1rq1bk1/Bnnb1p1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R1BQ1PP1/R5K1 b - - 7 26","n":"<p>26.Rea1. Two rooks on the a-file. Black's whole army is now tied to the queenside.</p>"},{"u":"d8e7","s":"Qe7","by":"b","f":"r1r2bk1/Bnnbqp1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R1BQ1PP1/R5K1 w - - 8 27"},{"u":"c2b1","s":"Bb1","by":"w","f":"r1r2bk1/Bnnbqp1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R2Q1PP1/RB4K1 b - - 9 27"},{"u":"d7e8","s":"Be8","by":"b","f":"r1r1bbk1/Bnn1qp1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2NNP/R2Q1PP1/RB4K1 w - - 10 28"},{"u":"g3e2","s":"Ne2","by":"w","f":"r1r1bbk1/Bnn1qp1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2N1P/R2QNPP1/RB4K1 b - - 11 28","n":"<p>28.Ne2 and next 29.Nh2: the knights start drifting toward the <b>other</b> side of the board.</p>"},{"u":"b7d8","s":"Nd8","by":"b","f":"r1rnbbk1/B1n1qp1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P2N1P/R2QNPP1/RB4K1 w - - 12 29"},{"u":"f3h2","s":"Nh2","by":"w","f":"r1rnbbk1/B1n1qp1p/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P4P/R2QNPPN/RB4K1 b - - 13 29"},{"u":"f8g7","s":"Bg7","by":"b","f":"r1rnb1k1/B1n1qpbp/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1P3/2P4P/R2QNPPN/RB4K1 w - - 14 30"},{"u":"f2f4","s":"f4","by":"w","f":"r1rnb1k1/B1n1qpbp/3p2p1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1PP2/2P4P/R2QN1PN/RB4K1 b - - 0 30","n":"<p><span class='mv'>30.f4!</span> The second front opens. Black's pieces are on the queenside, and they can't cross the locked centre quickly.</p>","a":[{"u":"f4f5","k":"a"}]},{"u":"f7f6","s":"f6","by":"b","f":"r1rnb1k1/B1n1q1bp/3p1pp1/1p1Pp3/1Pp1PP2/2P4P/R2QN1PN/RB4K1 w - - 0 31"},{"u":"f4f5","s":"f5","by":"w","f":"r1rnb1k1/B1n1q1bp/3p1pp1/1p1PpP2/1Pp1P3/2P4P/R2QN1PN/RB4K1 b - - 0 31","n":"<p><span class='mv'>31.f5</span> White gains kingside space. Black's king will be the second weakness.</p>"},{"u":"g6g5","s":"g5","by":"b","f":"r1rnb1k1/B1n1q1bp/3p1p2/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/2P4P/R2QN1PN/RB4K1 w - - 0 32"},{"u":"b1c2","s":"Bc2","by":"w","f":"r1rnb1k1/B1n1q1bp/3p1p2/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/2P4P/R1BQN1PN/R5K1 b - - 1 32"},{"u":"e8f7","s":"Bf7","by":"b","f":"r1rn2k1/B1n1qbbp/3p1p2/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/2P4P/R1BQN1PN/R5K1 w - - 2 33"},{"u":"e2g3","s":"Ng3","by":"w","f":"r1rn2k1/B1n1qbbp/3p1p2/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/2P3NP/R1BQ2PN/R5K1 b - - 3 33","n":"<p>33.Ng3. Every white piece now has a job on the kingside, while Black's knights (c7, b7) are still stuck on the queenside. The finish is in the case files.</p>","a":[{"u":"g3h5","k":"c"}],"m":["c7","b7"]},{"u":"d8b7","s":"Nb7","by":"b","f":"r1r3k1/Bnn1qbbp/3p1p2/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/2P3NP/R1BQ2PN/R5K1 w - - 4 34"}],"why":"<p>One weakness can be defended, because the defender just puts pieces next to it. The cure is a <b>second weakness far away</b>. Through a locked centre, the defender's pieces can't switch sides fast enough. The attacker isn't faster in general; he's faster <i>at switching</i>, because he prepared both fronts in advance.</p>","diff":0,"mate":false},{"id":"c-ba3","pattern":"skeleton","kind":"solved","title":"What the Pawns Built","src":"Botvinnik – Capablanca · AVRO 1938 (the finish)","fen":"8/p3q1kp/1p2Pnp1/3pQ3/2pP4/1nP3N1/1B4PP/6K1 w - - 5 30","orient":"w","n0":"<p>The pawn on e6 is the end product of the plan e4-e5-f4-f5. Black's queen on e7 is its only stopper.</p>","a0":[{"u":"e6e7","k":"a"},{"u":"e7e8","k":"a"}],"m0":["e6"],"steps":[{"u":"b2a3","s":"Ba3","by":"w","f":"8/p3q1kp/1p2Pnp1/3pQ3/2pP4/BnP3N1/6PP/6K1 b - - 6 30","n":"<p><span class='mv'>30.Ba3!!</span> Offering the bishop to drag the queen off e7 (a deflection, if you've read the tactics page).</p>","a":[{"u":"a3e7","k":"b"}]},{"u":"e7a3","s":"Qxa3","by":"b","f":"8/p5kp/1p2Pnp1/3pQ3/2pP4/qnP3N1/6PP/6K1 w - - 0 31","n":"<p>Capablanca takes. The blockade is gone.</p>"},{"u":"g3h5","s":"Nh5+","by":"w","f":"8/p5kp/1p2Pnp1/3pQ2N/2pP4/qnP5/6PP/6K1 b - - 1 31","n":"<p><span class='mv'>31.Nh5+!</span> Another sacrifice, opening the king.</p>"},{"u":"g6h5","s":"gxh5","by":"b","f":"8/p5kp/1p2Pn2/3pQ2p/2pP4/qnP5/6PP/6K1 w - - 0 32"},{"u":"e5g5","s":"Qg5+","by":"w","f":"8/p5kp/1p2Pn2/3p2Qp/2pP4/qnP5/6PP/6K1 b - - 1 32","n":"<p><span class='mv'>32.Qg5+</span></p>"},{"u":"g7f8","s":"Kf8","by":"b","f":"5k2/p6p/1p2Pn2/3p2Qp/2pP4/qnP5/6PP/6K1 w - - 2 33"},{"u":"g5f6","s":"Qxf6+","by":"w","f":"5k2/p6p/1p2PQ2/3p3p/2pP4/qnP5/6PP/6K1 b - - 0 33","n":"<p><span class='mv'>33.Qxf6+</span> The queen takes the knight that guarded e8.</p>"},{"u":"f8g8","s":"Kg8","by":"b","f":"6k1/p6p/1p2PQ2/3p3p/2pP4/qnP5/6PP/6K1 w - - 1 34"},{"u":"e6e7","s":"e7","by":"w","f":"6k1/p3P2p/1p3Q2/3p3p/2pP4/qnP5/6PP/6K1 b - - 0 34","n":"<p><span class='mv'>34.e7</span> The pawn marches on. Black has only checks left, and Botvinnik's king walked out of them. White won.</p>"}],"why":"<p>The brilliant sacrifices were only possible because of a pawn decision 16 moves earlier. Good structure produces tactics, while bad structure only produces hope.</p>","diff":3,"mate":false,"teaser":"Sixteen moves after Capablanca's ...c4, Botvinnik's pawn wave has produced a monster: a passed pawn on e6, one step from e7. Black's queen blockades it. Everything White does now is about that pawn."},{"id":"c-octopus","pattern":"outpost","kind":"solved","title":"The Octopus Strikes","src":"Karpov – Kasparov · World Championship 1985, game 16 (the finish)","fen":"2r1r3/3n1pk1/6bp/8/Np1q2P1/1P1n2P1/3Q2BP/1N1R1R1K b - - 2 32","orient":"b","n0":"<p>The octopus on d3 is still there. White's pieces are scattered around it.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["d3"],"steps":[{"u":"d7f6","s":"Nf6","by":"b","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/5nbp/8/Np1q2P1/1P1n2P1/3Q2BP/1N1R1R1K w - - 3 33","n":"<p>32...Nf6. The second knight comes to help.</p>"},{"u":"f1f4","s":"Rf4","by":"w","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/5nbp/8/Np1q1RP1/1P1n2P1/3Q2BP/1N1R3K b - - 4 33"},{"u":"f6e4","s":"Ne4","by":"b","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/6bp/8/Np1qnRP1/1P1n2P1/3Q2BP/1N1R3K w - - 5 34","n":"<p>33...Ne4. Both knights now point at f2 and the white king.</p>"},{"u":"d2d3","s":"Qxd3","by":"w","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/6bp/8/Np1qnRP1/1P1Q2P1/6BP/1N1R3K b - - 0 34","n":"<p>34.Qxd3. White finally takes the octopus...</p>"},{"u":"e4f2","s":"Nf2+","by":"b","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/6bp/8/Np1q1RP1/1P1Q2P1/5nBP/1N1R3K w - - 1 35","n":"<p><span class='mv'>34...Nf2+!</span> ...and the other knight forks king and queen.</p>","a":[{"u":"f2d3","k":"b"},{"u":"f2h1","k":"b"}]},{"u":"f4f2","s":"Rxf2","by":"w","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/6bp/8/Np1q2P1/1P1Q2P1/5RBP/1N1R3K b - - 0 35"},{"u":"g6d3","s":"Bxd3","by":"b","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/7p/8/Np1q2P1/1P1b2P1/5RBP/1N1R3K w - - 0 36","n":"<p>The bishop takes back on d3. Black has won the exchange and the attack goes on.</p>"},{"u":"f2d2","s":"Rfd2","by":"w","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/7p/8/Np1q2P1/1P1b2P1/3R2BP/1N1R3K b - - 1 36"},{"u":"d4e3","s":"Qe3","by":"b","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/7p/8/Np4P1/1P1bq1P1/3R2BP/1N1R3K w - - 2 37"},{"u":"d2d3","s":"Rxd3","by":"w","f":"2r1r3/5pk1/7p/8/Np4P1/1P1Rq1P1/6BP/1N1R3K b - - 0 37"},{"u":"c8c1","s":"Rc1","by":"b","f":"4r3/5pk1/7p/8/Np4P1/1P1Rq1P1/6BP/1NrR3K w - - 1 38"},{"u":"a4b2","s":"Nb2","by":"w","f":"4r3/5pk1/7p/8/1p4P1/1P1Rq1P1/1N4BP/1NrR3K b - - 2 38"},{"u":"e3f2","s":"Qf2","by":"b","f":"4r3/5pk1/7p/8/1p4P1/1P1R2P1/1N3qBP/1NrR3K w - - 3 39"},{"u":"b1d2","s":"Nd2","by":"w","f":"4r3/5pk1/7p/8/1p4P1/1P1R2P1/1N1N1qBP/2rR3K b - - 4 39"},{"u":"c1d1","s":"Rxd1+","by":"b","f":"4r3/5pk1/7p/8/1p4P1/1P1R2P1/1N1N1qBP/3r3K w - - 0 40"},{"u":"b2d1","s":"Nxd1","by":"w","f":"4r3/5pk1/7p/8/1p4P1/1P1R2P1/3N1qBP/3N3K b - - 0 40"},{"u":"e8e1","s":"Re1+","by":"b","f":"8/5pk1/7p/8/1p4P1/1P1R2P1/3N1qBP/3Nr2K w - - 1 41","n":"<p><span class='mv'>40...Re1+</span> White resigned. Kasparov led the match after this game, and won the title a few weeks later, the youngest undisputed champion until then.</p>"}],"why":"<p>An outpost piece ties down the enemy all game, and even when it's finally taken, the squares around it are still weak. White spent the whole game reacting to one knight.</p>","diff":3,"mate":false,"teaser":"The black knight on d3 has stood there for 16 moves. White has finally decided to take it. What does an outpost piece do when the opponent comes for it?"},{"id":"c-kingwalk","pattern":"worst","kind":"solved","title":"The King Is a Piece Too","src":"Fischer – Petrosian · Buenos Aires 1971, game 7 (continued)","fen":"r4k2/2Rn1ppp/p2r4/3pR3/1P6/3B1P2/P5PP/6K1 w - - 4 25","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Queens are gone, so the king no longer has to hide. On g1 it does nothing. It's White's worst piece.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["g1"],"steps":[{"u":"e5e2","s":"Re2","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2Rn1ppp/p2r4/3p4/1P6/3B1P2/P3R1PP/6K1 b - - 5 25","n":"<p>25.Re2. First the second rook gets a job.</p>"},{"u":"g7g6","s":"g6","by":"b","f":"r4k2/2Rn1p1p/p2r2p1/3p4/1P6/3B1P2/P3R1PP/6K1 w - - 0 26"},{"u":"g1f2","s":"Kf2","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2Rn1p1p/p2r2p1/3p4/1P6/3B1P2/P3RKPP/8 b - - 1 26","n":"<p><span class='mv'>26.Kf2</span> The king sets off toward the centre.</p>","a":[{"u":"f2e3","k":"a"}]},{"u":"h7h5","s":"h5","by":"b","f":"r4k2/2Rn1p2/p2r2p1/3p3p/1P6/3B1P2/P3RKPP/8 w - - 0 27"},{"u":"f3f4","s":"f4","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2Rn1p2/p2r2p1/3p3p/1P3P2/3B4/P3RKPP/8 b - - 0 27","n":"<p>27.f4. The pawn takes e5 and g5 away from Black's pieces.</p>"},{"u":"h5h4","s":"h4","by":"b","f":"r4k2/2Rn1p2/p2r2p1/3p4/1P3P1p/3B4/P3RKPP/8 w - - 0 28"},{"u":"f2f3","s":"Kf3","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2Rn1p2/p2r2p1/3p4/1P3P1p/3B1K2/P3R1PP/8 b - - 1 28","n":"<p><span class='mv'>28.Kf3</span></p>"},{"u":"f7f5","s":"f5","by":"b","f":"r4k2/2Rn4/p2r2p1/3p1p2/1P3P1p/3B1K2/P3R1PP/8 w - - 0 29"},{"u":"f3e3","s":"Ke3","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2Rn4/p2r2p1/3p1p2/1P3P1p/3BK3/P3R1PP/8 b - - 1 29","n":"<p><span class='mv'>29.Ke3</span> The king now stands where a knight would love to be.</p>"},{"u":"d5d4","s":"d4+","by":"b","f":"r4k2/2Rn4/p2r2p1/5p2/1P1p1P1p/3BK3/P3R1PP/8 w - - 0 30"},{"u":"e3d2","s":"Kd2","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2Rn4/p2r2p1/5p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2KR1PP/8 b - - 1 30","n":"<p>30.Kd2. The king sidesteps the pawn check. Every white piece is now active, and Black's pieces are tied up. Fischer's next decision is waiting for you in the Arena.</p>"},{"u":"d7b6","s":"Nb6","by":"b","f":"r4k2/2R5/pn1r2p1/5p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2KR1PP/8 w - - 2 31"}],"why":"<p>\"Improve your worst piece\" includes the king. Once the queens are off, the king becomes a strong fighting piece, often the best one on the board.</p>","diff":2,"mate":false,"teaser":"Fischer's rook is on the 7th, his bishop rules the light squares. Which white piece is doing nothing at all?"},{"id":"c-nd6","pattern":"outpost","kind":"solved","title":"The Hole on d6","src":"Alekhine – Nimzowitsch · San Remo 1930 (earlier)","fen":"r4rk1/pb1qnppp/1pn1p3/1N1pP3/PP1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R3KB1R w KQ - 1 14","orient":"w","n0":"<p>d6 is a hole: Black's e-pawn has passed it, and Black's c-pawn is gone. No black pawn can ever attack it, and Black's dark-squared bishop has been traded.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["d6"],"steps":[{"u":"b5d6","s":"Nd6","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/pb1qnppp/1pnNp3/3pP3/PP1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R3KB1R b KQ - 2 14","n":"<p><span class='mv'>14.Nd6</span> The knight jumps into the hole, attacking b7 and f7.</p>","a":[{"u":"d6b7","k":"a"},{"u":"d6f7","k":"a"}]},{"u":"f7f5","s":"f5","by":"b","f":"r4rk1/pb1qn1pp/1pnNp3/3pPp2/PP1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R3KB1R w KQ f6 0 15"},{"u":"a4a5","s":"a5","by":"w","f":"r4rk1/pb1qn1pp/1pnNp3/P2pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R3KB1R b KQ - 0 15","n":"<p>15.a5. Pressure on the queenside too.</p>"},{"u":"e7c8","s":"Nc8","by":"b","f":"r1n2rk1/pb1q2pp/1pnNp3/P2pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R3KB1R w KQ - 1 16","n":"<p>15...Nc8. Black's knight must come back just to challenge d6.</p>"},{"u":"d6b7","s":"Nxb7","by":"w","f":"r1n2rk1/pN1q2pp/1pn1p3/P2pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R3KB1R b KQ - 0 16","n":"<p>16.Nxb7. The knight trades itself for Black's light-squared bishop...</p>"},{"u":"d7b7","s":"Qxb7","by":"b","f":"r1n2rk1/pq4pp/1pn1p3/P2pPp2/1P1P1P2/5N2/3Q2PP/R3KB1R w KQ - 0 17","n":"<p>...and now White's light-squared bishop has no rival on the light squares. A few moves later it lands on b5 for good.</p>"}],"why":"<p>The pawn chain d4-e5 created a hole on d6 for White's pieces. Outposts are just the other face of the pawn skeleton: every pawn advance leaves squares behind it that it can never guard again.</p>","diff":2,"mate":false,"teaser":"It's a French Defence structure: White's pawns on d4 and e5, Black's on d5 and e6. Black has no dark-squared bishop any more. Look at d6."},{"id":"c-karpov-finish","pattern":"two","kind":"solved","title":"The Second Front Breaks","src":"Karpov – Unzicker · Nice 1974 (the finish)","fen":"r1rnq1k1/B1n2bb1/3p1p1p/1p1PpPpB/1Pp1P3/R1P1N1NP/R5P1/3Q2K1 w - - 12 41","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White's bishop on h5, knight on e3 and queen are all aimed at the kingside. Black's knights on c7 and d8 are far away.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["c7","d8"],"steps":[{"u":"h5f7","s":"Bxf7+","by":"w","f":"r1rnq1k1/B1n2Bb1/3p1p1p/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/R1P1N1NP/R5P1/3Q2K1 b - - 0 41","n":"<p><span class='mv'>41.Bxf7+</span> White trades off Black's best kingside defender.</p>"},{"u":"d8f7","s":"Nxf7","by":"b","f":"r1r1q1k1/B1n2nb1/3p1p1p/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/R1P1N1NP/R5P1/3Q2K1 w - - 0 42"},{"u":"d1h5","s":"Qh5","by":"w","f":"r1r1q1k1/B1n2nb1/3p1p1p/1p1PpPpQ/1Pp1P3/R1P1N1NP/R5P1/6K1 b - - 1 42","n":"<p><span class='mv'>42.Qh5</span> The queen walks into the light squares around the king.</p>"},{"u":"f7d8","s":"Nd8","by":"b","f":"r1rnq1k1/B1n3b1/3p1p1p/1p1PpPpQ/1Pp1P3/R1P1N1NP/R5P1/6K1 w - - 2 43"},{"u":"h5g6","s":"Qg6","by":"w","f":"r1rnq1k1/B1n3b1/3p1pQp/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/R1P1N1NP/R5P1/6K1 b - - 3 43","n":"<p><span class='mv'>43.Qg6</span></p>"},{"u":"g8f8","s":"Kf8","by":"b","f":"r1rnqk2/B1n3b1/3p1pQp/1p1PpPp1/1Pp1P3/R1P1N1NP/R5P1/6K1 w - - 4 44"},{"u":"g3h5","s":"Nh5","by":"w","f":"r1rnqk2/B1n3b1/3p1pQp/1p1PpPpN/1Pp1P3/R1P1N2P/R5P1/6K1 b - - 5 44","n":"<p><span class='mv'>44.Nh5</span> Black resigned. There's no defence against Qxg7 or Qxh6 with the queen and knight working together.</p>","a":[{"u":"h5g7","k":"b"},{"u":"g6h6","k":"b"}]}],"why":"<p>Black never had time to bring his queenside pieces across. The first front (the a-file) wasn't where the game was won. Its job was to keep Black's pieces busy.</p>","diff":3,"mate":false,"teaser":"For twenty moves Karpov held the queenside and built up on the kingside. Black's knights are still on the queenside. Now the second front opens."},{"id":"a-botvinnik","pattern":"skeleton","kind":"practice","title":"Botvinnik's Plan, Three Decisions","src":"Botvinnik – Capablanca · AVRO 1938","fen":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/1p3n2/3p4/P1pP4/1nP1PPN1/1BQ3PP/4RRK1 w - - 1 19","orient":"w","n0":"","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"e3e4","s":"e4","by":"w","user":true,"choices":[{"u":"e3e4","s":"e4","ok":true,"why":"Yes. The central majority (d4, e3, f3 vs d5) goes forward while Black's queen is off grabbing a pawn on a4. Engine: about +0.45."},{"u":"h2h3","s":"h3","ok":false,"why":"A safe 'useful' move, but your pawn majority does nothing. Black will consolidate the extra queenside pawn. Engine: about −0.2."},{"u":"g3f5","s":"Nf5","ok":false,"why":"The knight hop attacks nothing that matters and doesn't change the structure. Engine: about −0.3."}],"f":"r3r1k1/p2q1ppp/1p3n2/3p4/P1pPP3/1nP2PN1/1BQ3PP/4RRK1 b - - 0 19","n":"<p><span class='mv'>19.e4</span> The centre opens.</p>"},{"u":"d7a4","s":"Qxa4","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p4ppp/1p3n2/3p4/q1pPP3/1nP2PN1/1BQ3PP/4RRK1 w - - 0 20","n":"<p>Capablanca takes the a4 pawn as planned.</p>"},{"u":"e4e5","s":"e5","by":"w","f":"r3r1k1/p4ppp/1p3n2/3pP3/q1pP4/1nP2PN1/1BQ3PP/4RRK1 b - - 0 20","n":"<p>20.e5. The pawn kicks the f6 knight away from the kingside.</p>"},{"u":"f6d7","s":"Nd7","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p2n1ppp/1p6/3pP3/q1pP4/1nP2PN1/1BQ3PP/4RRK1 w - - 1 21","n":"<p>20...Nd7 retreats.</p>"},{"u":"c2f2","s":"Qf2","by":"w","f":"r3r1k1/p2n1ppp/1p6/3pP3/q1pP4/1nP2PN1/1B3QPP/4RRK1 b - - 2 21","n":"<p>21.Qf2 eyes the f-pawn's advance.</p>"},{"u":"g7g6","s":"g6","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p2n1p1p/1p4p1/3pP3/q1pP4/1nP2PN1/1B3QPP/4RRK1 w - - 0 22"},{"u":"f3f4","s":"f4","by":"w","f":"r3r1k1/p2n1p1p/1p4p1/3pP3/q1pP1P2/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4RRK1 b - - 0 22","n":"<p>22.f4. The second pawn joins the wave.</p>"},{"u":"f7f5","s":"f5","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p2n3p/1p4p1/3pPp2/q1pP1P2/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4RRK1 w - f6 0 23","n":"<p>22...f5 tries to block it.</p>"},{"u":"e5f6","s":"exf6","by":"w","f":"r3r1k1/p2n3p/1p3Pp1/3p4/q1pP1P2/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4RRK1 b - - 0 23","n":"<p>23.exf6, taking en passant.</p>"},{"u":"d7f6","s":"Nxf6","by":"b","f":"r3r1k1/p6p/1p3np1/3p4/q1pP1P2/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4RRK1 w - - 0 24"},{"u":"f4f5","s":"f5","by":"w","user":true,"choices":[{"u":"f4f5","s":"f5","ok":true,"why":"The wave keeps rolling: f5 pries open the black king and makes your e-pawn a monster. Engine: about +0.5."},{"u":"h2h3","s":"h3","ok":false,"why":"Waiting lets Black blockade the e-file and stabilise. Engine: about 0.0."},{"u":"e1e8","s":"Rxe8+","ok":false,"why":"Trading off your active rook releases the pressure your structure created. Engine: about −0.3."}],"f":"r3r1k1/p6p/1p3np1/3p1P2/q1pP4/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4RRK1 b - - 0 24","n":"<p><span class='mv'>24.f5</span></p>"},{"u":"e8e1","s":"Rxe1","by":"b","f":"r5k1/p6p/1p3np1/3p1P2/q1pP4/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4rRK1 w - - 0 25"},{"u":"f1e1","s":"Rxe1","by":"w","f":"r5k1/p6p/1p3np1/3p1P2/q1pP4/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4R1K1 b - - 0 25","n":"<p>25.Rxe1</p>"},{"u":"a8e8","s":"Re8","by":"b","f":"4r1k1/p6p/1p3np1/3p1P2/q1pP4/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/4R1K1 w - - 1 26","n":"<p>25...Re8</p>"},{"u":"e1e6","s":"Re6","by":"w","user":true,"choices":[{"u":"e1e6","s":"Re6","ok":true,"why":"The rook goes to e6, an outpost protected by the f5 pawn, deep in Black's half, in front of the soon-to-be passed pawn. Engine: about +0.5."},{"u":"h2h4","s":"h4","ok":false,"why":"Black answers ...Ng4 and the knight starts harassing your queen and king. Engine: about −1.5."},{"u":"f2e2","s":"Qe2","ok":false,"why":"The queen steps in front of Black's e8-rook: ...Rxe2 wins the queen for a rook. Engine: about −4."}],"f":"4r1k1/p6p/1p2Rnp1/3p1P2/q1pP4/1nP3N1/1B3QPP/6K1 b - - 2 26","n":"<p><span class='mv'>26.Re6</span> And four moves later came 30.Ba3!!, the brilliant finish in the case files.</p>"}],"why":"<p>Three decisions, one idea: <b>the pawns decide where to play</b>. ...c4 handed White the centre, and every white move after that pushed the central majority forward.</p>","diff":3,"mate":false,"task":"You are Botvinnik. Capablanca has just played ...Nb3 and will win your a4 pawn. Make the three key decisions of this game.","user":"w"},{"id":"a-fischer-7th","pattern":"files","kind":"practice","title":"Pigs on the Seventh","src":"Fischer – Petrosian · Buenos Aires 1971, game 7","fen":"r4k2/2R5/pn1r2p1/5p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2KR1PP/8 w - - 2 31","orient":"w","n0":"","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"e2e7","s":"Ree7","by":"w","user":true,"choices":[{"u":"e2e7","s":"Ree7","ok":true,"why":"Both rooks on the 7th: Black's king and pawns are attacked from the side, where pawns can't protect each other. Engine: about +1.0."},{"u":"d3c4","s":"Bc4","ok":false,"why":"This gives Black ...Nxc4+, trading off your bishop, the piece that fits the light-squared pawns. Engine: about +0.7."},{"u":"d2c1","s":"Kc1","ok":false,"why":"Too passive. Black gets ...Nd5 and his rooks come alive. Engine: about −0.2."}],"f":"r4k2/2R1R3/pn1r2p1/5p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2K2PP/8 b - - 3 31","n":"<p><span class='mv'>31.Ree7</span> Two rooks on the 7th, nicknamed <b>pigs on the seventh</b> because they eat everything.</p>"},{"u":"b6d5","s":"Nd5","by":"b","f":"r4k2/2R1R3/p2r2p1/3n1p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2K2PP/8 w - - 4 32"},{"u":"e7f7","s":"Rf7+","by":"w","f":"r4k2/2R2R2/p2r2p1/3n1p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2K2PP/8 b - - 5 32","n":"<p>32.Rf7+ checks the king toward the corner...</p>"},{"u":"f8e8","s":"Ke8","by":"b","f":"r3k3/2R2R2/p2r2p1/3n1p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2K2PP/8 w - - 6 33"},{"u":"c7b7","s":"Rb7","by":"w","f":"r3k3/1R3R2/p2r2p1/3n1p2/1P1p1P1p/3B4/P2K2PP/8 b - - 7 33","n":"<p>33.Rb7 attacks along the rank...</p>"},{"u":"d5b4","s":"Nxb4","by":"b","f":"r3k3/1R3R2/p2r2p1/5p2/1n1p1P1p/3B4/P2K2PP/8 w - - 0 34"},{"u":"d3c4","s":"Bc4","by":"w","f":"r3k3/1R3R2/p2r2p1/5p2/1nBp1P1p/8/P2K2PP/8 b - - 1 34","n":"<p>34.Bc4. Petrosian resigned: the rooks on the 7th and the bishop weave a net he can't escape.</p>"}],"why":"<p>On the 7th rank, a rook attacks pawns from the side, where other pawns can't defend them, and it boxes the king in on the back rank. Two rooks there are usually decisive.</p>","diff":2,"mate":false,"task":"You are Fischer. One rook is already on the 7th rank. What now?","user":"w"},{"id":"a-outpost","pattern":"outpost","kind":"practice","title":"The Sicilian Hole","src":"Study position","fen":"2r2rk1/2qnbppp/p2p4/1p2p3/4P3/2N3P1/PPP1QPBP/3R1RK1 w - - 0 16","orient":"w","n0":"","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"c3d5","s":"Nd5","by":"w","user":true,"choices":[{"u":"c3d5","s":"Nd5","ok":true,"why":"d5 is a hole: Black's c-pawn is gone and the e-pawn has passed it. The knight lands with tempo on the queen and the e7-bishop. Engine: about +0.5."},{"u":"a2a3","s":"a3","ok":false,"why":"A decent move in general, but it ignores the most important square on the board. Engine: about +0.1."},{"u":"c3b1","s":"Nb1","ok":false,"why":"This retreats from the hole and leaves c2 hanging: ...Qxc2. Engine: about −0.45."}],"f":"2r2rk1/2qnbppp/p2p4/1p1Np3/4P3/6P1/PPP1QPBP/3R1RK1 b - - 1 16","n":"<p><span class='mv'>16.Nd5</span> The knight lands in the hole with tempo.</p>"},{"u":"c7d8","s":"Qd8","by":"b","f":"2rq1rk1/3nbppp/p2p4/1p1Np3/4P3/6P1/PPP1QPBP/3R1RK1 w - - 2 17","n":"<p>The queen steps back.</p>"},{"u":"g2h3","s":"Bh3","by":"w","f":"2rq1rk1/3nbppp/p2p4/1p1Np3/4P3/6PB/PPP1QP1P/3R1RK1 b - - 3 17","n":"<p><span class='mv'>17.Bh3</span> Next idea: trade off the d7-knight, the only black piece that could ever challenge d5.</p>"}],"why":"<p>The most valuable square on the board is often one that's <b>empty</b>: a hole the enemy pawns have left behind. Put a knight on it and remove whatever could challenge it.</p>","diff":2,"mate":false,"task":"Black's pawns stand on d6 and e5. One square in front of them can never be attacked by a black pawn again. Find the best home for your knight.","user":"w"},{"id":"a-fit","pattern":"fit","kind":"practice","title":"Good Knight, Bad Bishop","src":"Study position","fen":"2r3k1/3b1p2/p3p1p1/1p1p4/3N4/4P2P/PP3PP1/2R3K1 w - - 0 25","orient":"w","n0":"","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"c1c8","s":"Rxc8+","by":"w","user":true,"choices":[{"u":"c1c8","s":"Rxc8+","ok":true,"why":"Yes. Trade the rooks and you get your strong knight against a bishop that's blocked by its own pawns. Black's light-square pawns block the bishop, and the dark squares (c5, e5) are holes for your knight and king. Engine: about +0.35."},{"u":"c1c2","s":"Rc2","ok":false,"why":"Black just takes (...Rxc2 Nxc2) and your knight is pushed back to a passive square. You get the same pieces in a worse version. Engine: about −0.1."},{"u":"c1d1","s":"Rd1","ok":false,"why":"This avoids the trade, so Black keeps his rook to cover the dark squares and activates his king. Engine: about −0.4."}],"f":"2R3k1/3b1p2/p3p1p1/1p1p4/3N4/4P2P/PP3PP1/6K1 b - - 0 25","n":"<p><span class='mv'>25.Rxc8+</span> Rooks off.</p>"},{"u":"d7c8","s":"Bxc8","by":"b","f":"2b3k1/5p2/p3p1p1/1p1p4/3N4/4P2P/PP3PP1/6K1 w - - 0 26","n":"<p>The bishop recaptures, and now it's stuck behind its own pawns.</p>"},{"u":"f2f4","s":"f4","by":"w","f":"2b3k1/5p2/p3p1p1/1p1p4/3N1P2/4P2P/PP4P1/6K1 b - - 0 26","n":"<p>26.f4. White's pawns go to <b>dark</b> squares, where the bishop can never attack them.</p>"},{"u":"f7f6","s":"f6","by":"b","f":"2b3k1/8/p3ppp1/1p1p4/3N1P2/4P2P/PP4P1/6K1 w - - 0 27"},{"u":"g1f2","s":"Kf2","by":"w","f":"2b3k1/8/p3ppp1/1p1p4/3N1P2/4P2P/PP3KP1/8 b - - 1 27","n":"<p>27.Kf2. Next the king walks toward d4 and the dark-square holes. Black's bishop can only watch.</p>"}],"why":"<p>A bad bishop is one whose own pawns stand on its colour. Against it, put your pawns on the <b>other</b> colour and your pieces on the holes it can't reach. Trading the rooks removed the only black piece that could cover the dark squares.</p>","diff":2,"mate":false,"task":"Material is equal. Black's pawns on a6, b5, d5, e6, f7 and g6 all sit on light squares, the same colour as his bishop. Choose the trade that leaves the right minor pieces on the board.","user":"w"},{"id":"a-worst","pattern":"worst","kind":"practice","title":"The Knight Still in Bed","src":"Study position","fen":"2r2rk1/2qnbppp/p2p4/1p2p3/4P3/6P1/PPP1QPBP/RN3RK1 w - - 0 16","orient":"w","n0":"","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"b1c3","s":"Nc3","by":"w","user":true,"choices":[{"u":"b1c3","s":"Nc3","ok":true,"why":"Yes. The sleeping knight wakes up, heads for the d5 hole and frees the a1-rook. Engine: about +0.1."},{"u":"f1d1","s":"Rd1","ok":false,"why":"A nice-looking rook move, but the b1-knight is still asleep, and Black grabs c2: ...Qxc2. Engine: about −0.55."},{"u":"a2a3","s":"a3","ok":false,"why":"A pawn move while a whole knight and rook are still undeveloped. Black takes on c2. Engine: about −0.85."}],"f":"2r2rk1/2qnbppp/p2p4/1p2p3/4P3/2N3P1/PPP1QPBP/R4RK1 b - - 1 16","n":"<p><span class='mv'>16.Nc3</span> The worst piece gets a job, and the a1-rook is connected at last.</p>"},{"u":"d7f6","s":"Nf6","by":"b","f":"2r2rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/1p2p3/4P3/2N3P1/PPP1QPBP/R4RK1 w - - 2 17","n":"<p>Black immediately covers d5 with ...Nf6: he knows where the knight wants to go.</p>"},{"u":"a2a3","s":"a3","by":"w","f":"2r2rk1/2q1bppp/p2p1n2/1p2p3/4P3/P1N3P1/1PP1QPBP/R4RK1 b - - 0 17","n":"<p>17.a3, preventing ...b4 (which would kick the knight off c3).</p>"},{"u":"c7d7","s":"Qd7","by":"b","f":"2r2rk1/3qbppp/p2p1n2/1p2p3/4P3/P1N3P1/1PP1QPBP/R4RK1 w - - 1 18"},{"u":"f1d1","s":"Rfd1","by":"w","f":"2r2rk1/3qbppp/p2p1n2/1p2p3/4P3/P1N3P1/1PP1QPBP/R2R2K1 b - - 2 18","n":"<p>18.Rfd1. Every white piece now has a job, and the knight's road to d5 is next.</p>"}],"why":"<p>Before attacking anything, ask which piece is doing nothing. A knight on b1 doesn't just do nothing: it also blocks a rook. Waking it up improves <b>two</b> pieces in one move.</p>","diff":1,"mate":false,"task":"It's move 16 and one white piece has never moved. Black is eyeing c2. Pick the move that fixes your position's weakest link.","user":"w"}]};
